1
00:00:07,020 --> 00:00:14,050
Listen. Practice. Speak. Practice makes you perfect.

2
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:22,050
Hello and welcome back to Business English Podcast. Hi everyone. Welcome to today's English Conversation Practice. I'm Daniel.

3
00:00:22,050 --> 00:00:36,050
And I'm Sophia. And we're really happy you're here learning with us today. On this channel, we help you speak English naturally and confidently through real conversations, practical examples, and everyday workplace situations.

4
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:41,330
That's right, and today we have a very important and practical topic, especially for anyone

5
00:00:41,330 --> 00:00:43,130
preparing for a job interview.

6
00:00:43,130 --> 00:00:49,330
Yes, today's topic is how to explain a project you worked on in 30 minutes at an interview.

7
00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:52,250
This is a very common interview question.

8
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:56,010
Employers often ask, can you tell me about a project you worked on?

9
00:00:56,010 --> 00:00:59,530
And many people feel nervous when they hear that question, because they don't know

10
00:00:59,530 --> 00:01:01,770
how to organize their answer.

11
00:01:01,770 --> 00:01:02,770
Exactly.

12
00:01:02,770 --> 00:01:07,730
have great experience, but if you cannot explain it clearly, the interviewer may not understand

13
00:01:07,730 --> 00:01:08,730
your value.

14
00:01:08,730 --> 00:01:14,170
So today, we're going to have a long, detailed conversation to help you understand exactly

15
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:17,250
how to explain your project step by step.

16
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:21,890
We'll walk through a realistic interview-style conversation, and we'll also break down the

17
00:01:21,890 --> 00:01:23,890
thinking behind each answer.

18
00:01:23,890 --> 00:01:25,570
So let's begin.

19
00:01:25,570 --> 00:01:26,930
Imagine this situation.

20
00:01:26,930 --> 00:01:30,650
Daniel is in a job interview, and I am the interviewer.

21
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:31,650
Sounds good.

22
00:01:31,650 --> 00:01:32,650
Let's start.

23
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:36,330
Daniel, can you tell me about a project you worked on recently?

24
00:01:36,330 --> 00:01:37,330
Yes, of course.

25
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:38,330
Thank you for the question.

26
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:43,010
I'd like to talk about a project I worked on at my previous company, where we focused

27
00:01:43,010 --> 00:01:46,530
on improving the company's online sales performance.

28
00:01:46,530 --> 00:01:47,730
That sounds interesting.

29
00:01:47,730 --> 00:01:50,930
Can you give me a bit more background about the project?

30
00:01:50,930 --> 00:01:51,930
Sure.

31
00:01:51,930 --> 00:01:56,730
At that time, our company had an e-commerce platform, but we noticed that sales growth

32
00:01:56,730 --> 00:02:00,290
had slowed down significantly over the past year.

33
00:02:00,290 --> 00:02:04,890
Customer engagement was also decreasing and our conversion rate was lower than industry

34
00:02:04,890 --> 00:02:05,890
benchmarks.

35
00:02:05,890 --> 00:02:06,890
I see.

36
00:02:06,890 --> 00:02:09,130
So there was a clear problem to solve.

37
00:02:09,130 --> 00:02:10,130
Exactly.

38
00:02:10,130 --> 00:02:14,810
The management team wanted to understand the reasons behind the decline and find ways

39
00:02:14,810 --> 00:02:16,490
to improve performance.

40
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:18,970
And what was the main goal of the project?

41
00:02:18,970 --> 00:02:24,330
The main goal was to increase the conversion rate by at least 20% within three months

42
00:02:24,330 --> 00:02:28,090
and improve overall customer engagement on the platform.

43
00:02:28,090 --> 00:02:30,610
That's a very clear and measurable goal.

44
00:02:30,610 --> 00:02:32,510
What was your role in this project?

45
00:02:32,510 --> 00:02:38,290
I was working as a project lead, so I was responsible for coordinating the team, analyzing

46
00:02:38,290 --> 00:02:42,210
data, and ensuring that we stayed on track with our timeline.

47
00:02:42,210 --> 00:02:44,330
What kind of team were you working with?

48
00:02:44,330 --> 00:02:46,310
It was a cross-functional team.

49
00:02:46,310 --> 00:02:52,470
We had members from marketing, IT, data analysis, and customer experience departments.

50
00:02:52,470 --> 00:02:54,650
That sounds like a complex setup.

51
00:02:54,650 --> 00:02:56,410
How did you begin the project?

52
00:02:56,410 --> 00:02:59,370
We started with a research and analysis phase.

53
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:05,270
First, we collected data on user behavior, including page visits, bounce rates, and customer

54
00:03:05,270 --> 00:03:06,430
feedback.

55
00:03:06,430 --> 00:03:08,090
What did you learn from that data?

56
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:12,810
We discovered that many users were leaving the website during the checkout process.

57
00:03:12,810 --> 00:03:18,210
There were also complaints about slow loading times and a complicated user interface.

58
00:03:18,210 --> 00:03:20,650
So usability was a key issue?

59
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:22,330
Yes, exactly.

60
00:03:22,330 --> 00:03:26,710
We realized that the user experience needed significant improvement.

61
00:03:26,710 --> 00:03:29,330
What did you do after identifying the problem?

62
00:03:29,330 --> 00:03:32,130
We moved into the solution design phase.

63
00:03:32,130 --> 00:03:37,290
We brainstormed several ideas, including simplifying the checkout process, improving

64
00:03:37,290 --> 00:03:41,070
website speed, and redesigning the user interface.

65
00:03:41,070 --> 00:03:43,870
How did you decide which solutions to implement?

66
00:03:43,870 --> 00:03:48,030
We prioritized solutions based on impact and feasibility.

67
00:03:48,030 --> 00:03:53,250
For example, improving the checkout process was a high priority because it directly affected

68
00:03:53,250 --> 00:03:54,250
conversions.

69
00:03:54,250 --> 00:03:55,550
That makes sense.

70
00:03:55,550 --> 00:03:58,270
Did you face any challenges during this phase?

71
00:03:58,270 --> 00:03:59,270
Yes.

72
00:03:59,270 --> 00:04:02,510
One of the main challenges was aligning different teams.

73
00:04:02,510 --> 00:04:06,790
Each department had its own priorities, and sometimes there were disagreements.

74
00:04:06,790 --> 00:04:08,030
How did you handle that?

75
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:12,310
I organized regular meetings where everyone could share their perspectives.

76
00:04:12,310 --> 00:04:16,550
We focused on the overall goal rather than individual preferences.

77
00:04:16,550 --> 00:04:18,630
That sounds like strong leadership.

78
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:20,070
What happened next?

79
00:04:20,070 --> 00:04:24,990
After finalizing the plan, the IT team began implementing the technical changes, while the

80
00:04:24,990 --> 00:04:27,710
marketing team prepared new campaigns.

81
00:04:27,710 --> 00:04:30,070
Did you test the changes before launching?

82
00:04:30,070 --> 00:04:32,770
Yes, we conducted A-B testing.

83
00:04:32,770 --> 00:04:37,990
We tested the new design with a group of users while keeping the old version for comparison.

84
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:39,790
What were the results of the testing?

85
00:04:39,790 --> 00:04:42,590
The new version showed a significant improvement.

86
00:04:42,590 --> 00:04:46,430
The checkout completion rate increased by about 25%.

87
00:04:46,430 --> 00:04:47,710
That's impressive.

88
00:04:47,710 --> 00:04:49,710
Did you then implement it fully?

89
00:04:49,710 --> 00:04:50,430
Yes.

90
00:04:50,430 --> 00:04:53,350
After successful testing, we rolled out the changes

91
00:04:53,350 --> 00:04:54,710
to all users.

92
00:04:54,710 --> 00:04:57,430
And what were the final results of the project?

93
00:04:57,430 --> 00:04:59,870
The final results were very positive.

94
00:04:59,870 --> 00:05:02,910
We exceeded our target and increased the conversion rate

95
00:05:02,910 --> 00:05:04,670
by 28%.

96
00:05:04,670 --> 00:05:06,910
Customer satisfaction also improved

97
00:05:06,910 --> 00:05:08,750
based on feedback surveys.

98
00:05:08,750 --> 00:05:10,110
That's excellent.

99
00:05:10,110 --> 00:05:12,910
What did you personally learn from this project?

100
00:05:12,910 --> 00:05:15,870
I learned the importance of data-driven decision making

101
00:05:15,870 --> 00:05:18,550
and effective communication across teams.

102
00:05:18,550 --> 00:05:20,550
If you had to do the project again,

103
00:05:20,550 --> 00:05:22,350
would you do anything differently?

104
00:05:22,350 --> 00:05:26,330
Yes, I would involve the customer support team earlier.

105
00:05:26,330 --> 00:05:27,870
They had valuable insights

106
00:05:27,870 --> 00:05:30,590
that could have helped us identify issues faster.

107
00:05:30,590 --> 00:05:32,910
That's a very thoughtful reflection.

108
00:05:32,910 --> 00:05:33,750
Thank you.

109
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:35,350
Now let's pause for a moment

110
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:38,070
and explain why this answer is strong.

111
00:05:38,070 --> 00:05:39,710
Yes, that's important.

112
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:42,630
Notice how the answer followed a clear structure.

113
00:05:42,630 --> 00:05:45,710
First, Daniel introduced the project clearly.

114
00:05:45,710 --> 00:05:48,310
Then he explained the problem and the goal.

115
00:05:48,310 --> 00:05:51,310
After that, he described his role and the team.

116
00:05:51,310 --> 00:05:54,390
Then he talked about the process step by step.

117
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:57,750
He also included challenges and how he solved them.

118
00:05:57,750 --> 00:06:00,750
And finally, he shared measurable results.

119
00:06:00,750 --> 00:06:03,630
This is exactly what interviewers want to hear.

120
00:06:03,630 --> 00:06:06,630
Let's continue with more interview-style questions.

121
00:06:06,630 --> 00:06:10,190
Daniel, how did you manage your time during this project?

122
00:06:10,190 --> 00:06:12,670
I created a detailed project timeline

123
00:06:12,670 --> 00:06:14,350
with clear milestones.

124
00:06:14,350 --> 00:06:16,910
We used weekly check-ins to track progress

125
00:06:16,910 --> 00:06:18,870
and adjust plans if necessary.

126
00:06:18,870 --> 00:06:20,670
Did you ever face delays?

127
00:06:20,670 --> 00:06:23,670
Yes, there was a delay during the development phase

128
00:06:23,670 --> 00:06:25,230
due to technical issues.

129
00:06:25,230 --> 00:06:27,190
However, we adjusted our schedule

130
00:06:27,190 --> 00:06:29,190
and focused on critical tasks.

131
00:06:29,190 --> 00:06:30,830
That shows flexibility.

132
00:06:30,830 --> 00:06:33,830
How did you ensure good communication in the team?

133
00:06:33,830 --> 00:06:36,510
I used regular meetings, shared documents

134
00:06:36,510 --> 00:06:38,030
and clear reporting.

135
00:06:38,030 --> 00:06:41,510
I also made sure everyone understood their responsibilities.

136
00:06:41,510 --> 00:06:43,790
That's very important in teamwork.

137
00:06:43,790 --> 00:06:47,150
Yes, communication can make or break a project.

138
00:06:47,150 --> 00:06:50,790
Now let's make this even more practical for our listeners.

139
00:06:50,790 --> 00:06:51,710
Good idea.

140
00:06:51,710 --> 00:06:55,010
Let's simulate a shorter, more direct interview answer.

141
00:06:55,010 --> 00:06:58,510
Daniel, can you briefly explain a project you worked on?

142
00:06:58,510 --> 00:06:59,470
Sure.

143
00:06:59,470 --> 00:07:02,550
I worked on a project to improve our company's e-commerce

144
00:07:02,550 --> 00:07:03,470
platform.

145
00:07:03,470 --> 00:07:06,390
My role was to lead a cross-functional team.

146
00:07:06,390 --> 00:07:09,550
We analyzed customer data, identified issues

147
00:07:09,550 --> 00:07:12,790
in the checkout process, and implemented improvements.

148
00:07:12,790 --> 00:07:16,830
As a result, we increased our conversion rate by 28%.

149
00:07:16,830 --> 00:07:18,950
That's a great, concise answer.

150
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:21,710
Yes, and this is useful when time is limited.

151
00:07:21,710 --> 00:07:25,790
Now, let's expand again and go deeper into storytelling.

152
00:07:25,790 --> 00:07:27,470
Because in a 30-minute interview,

153
00:07:27,470 --> 00:07:29,750
you may need to explain more details.

154
00:07:29,750 --> 00:07:30,710
Exactly.

155
00:07:30,710 --> 00:07:32,630
Let's continue the conversation.

156
00:07:32,630 --> 00:07:35,590
Daniel, can you walk me through a difficult moment

157
00:07:35,590 --> 00:07:36,630
in the project?

158
00:07:36,630 --> 00:07:37,470
Yes.

159
00:07:37,470 --> 00:07:40,510
One difficult moment was when the IT team reported

160
00:07:40,510 --> 00:07:43,510
that the new design might affect system stability.

161
00:07:43,510 --> 00:07:45,110
That sounds serious.

162
00:07:45,110 --> 00:07:46,150
It was.

163
00:07:46,150 --> 00:07:48,470
We had to decide whether to delay the launch

164
00:07:48,470 --> 00:07:50,190
or simplify the changes.

165
00:07:50,190 --> 00:07:51,270
What did you do?

166
00:07:51,270 --> 00:07:53,710
I organized a meeting with all stakeholders

167
00:07:53,710 --> 00:07:55,510
and evaluated the risks.

168
00:07:55,510 --> 00:07:59,310
We decided to simplify certain features to ensure stability.

169
00:07:59,310 --> 00:08:01,510
That's a good decision-making process.

170
00:08:01,510 --> 00:08:02,390
Thank you.

171
00:08:02,390 --> 00:08:05,750
It taught me how to balance innovation with practicality.

172
00:08:05,750 --> 00:08:07,390
That's a valuable skill.

173
00:08:07,390 --> 00:08:10,490
Yes, especially in real business situations.

174
00:08:10,490 --> 00:08:12,930
Now let's talk about confidence.

175
00:08:12,930 --> 00:08:16,170
Many learners feel nervous when explaining projects.

176
00:08:16,170 --> 00:08:17,250
That's true.

177
00:08:17,250 --> 00:08:19,050
What advice would you give?

178
00:08:19,050 --> 00:08:22,490
I would say focus on clarity, not perfection.

179
00:08:22,490 --> 00:08:25,050
Speak slowly and organize your thoughts.

180
00:08:25,050 --> 00:08:26,710
That's excellent advice.

181
00:08:26,710 --> 00:08:29,890
Also, practice telling your project like a story.

182
00:08:29,890 --> 00:08:31,850
Beginning, middle, and end.

183
00:08:31,850 --> 00:08:32,890
Exactly.

184
00:08:32,890 --> 00:08:36,290
Let's continue with one final extended example.

185
00:08:36,290 --> 00:08:39,610
Daniel, imagine this is your final answer in an interview.

186
00:08:39,610 --> 00:08:41,930
Please give a complete explanation.

187
00:08:41,930 --> 00:08:42,890
Sure.

188
00:08:42,890 --> 00:08:44,570
One project I'd like to highlight

189
00:08:44,570 --> 00:08:46,650
is when I led an initiative to improve

190
00:08:46,650 --> 00:08:49,130
our company's online sales platform.

191
00:08:49,130 --> 00:08:50,850
At the time, we were experiencing

192
00:08:50,850 --> 00:08:54,090
declining conversion rates and customer engagement.

193
00:08:54,090 --> 00:08:56,850
The goal of the project was to increase conversions

194
00:08:56,850 --> 00:08:59,330
by 20% within three months.

195
00:08:59,330 --> 00:09:02,050
My role was to lead a cross-functional team

196
00:09:02,050 --> 00:09:04,610
and coordinate efforts across departments.

197
00:09:04,610 --> 00:09:07,090
We began by analyzing customer data

198
00:09:07,090 --> 00:09:09,130
and identifying key issues,

199
00:09:09,130 --> 00:09:11,610
including a complicated checkout process

200
00:09:11,610 --> 00:09:13,770
and slow website performance.

201
00:09:13,770 --> 00:09:15,090
Based on our findings,

202
00:09:15,090 --> 00:09:17,250
we redesigned the user interface

203
00:09:17,250 --> 00:09:19,450
and simplified the checkout steps.

204
00:09:19,450 --> 00:09:20,890
One of the biggest challenges

205
00:09:20,890 --> 00:09:22,730
was aligning different teams,

206
00:09:22,730 --> 00:09:25,650
but I addressed this by organizing regular meetings

207
00:09:25,650 --> 00:09:27,870
and focusing on shared goals.

208
00:09:27,870 --> 00:09:29,570
After implementing the changes,

209
00:09:29,570 --> 00:09:31,370
we conducted A-B testing

210
00:09:31,370 --> 00:09:33,570
and saw significant improvements.

211
00:09:33,570 --> 00:09:37,330
In the end, we increased the conversion rate by 28%

212
00:09:37,330 --> 00:09:39,730
and improved customer satisfaction.

213
00:09:39,730 --> 00:09:45,490
This project taught me the importance of teamwork, communication, and data-driven decisions.

214
00:09:45,490 --> 00:09:47,570
That was an excellent answer.

215
00:09:47,570 --> 00:09:48,370
Thank you.

216
00:09:48,370 --> 00:09:53,090
And that's exactly the level of detail you should aim for in a 30-minute interview.

217
00:09:53,090 --> 00:09:56,210
Yes, clear, structured, and confident.

218
00:09:56,210 --> 00:09:58,850
Before we finish, here's a question for you.

219
00:09:58,850 --> 00:10:01,330
Can you describe a project you worked on?

220
00:10:01,330 --> 00:10:06,050
Try to include the background, your role, the challenges, and the results.

221
00:10:06,050 --> 00:10:11,170
practice speaking it out loud. The more you practice, the more confident you'll become.

222
00:10:11,170 --> 00:10:15,330
Thank you so much for listening. We hope this long conversation helps you

223
00:10:15,330 --> 00:10:21,090
succeed in your next interview. Keep practicing, keep improving, and keep speaking English.

224
00:10:21,090 --> 00:10:24,290
See you in the next episode. Goodbye, everyone. Bye.

225
00:10:24,290 --> 00:10:27,650
Hello and welcome back to Business English Podcast.

226
00:10:27,650 --> 00:10:32,610
Hi, everyone. Welcome to today's English Conversation Practice. I'm Daniel.

227
00:10:32,610 --> 00:10:37,170
And I'm Sophia, and we're really happy you're here learning with us today.

228
00:10:37,170 --> 00:10:43,170
On this channel, we help you speak English naturally and confidently through real conversations,

229
00:10:43,170 --> 00:10:47,170
practical examples, and everyday workplace situations.

230
00:10:47,170 --> 00:10:52,930
Yes, and today's topic is extremely important for anyone preparing for job interviews or

231
00:10:52,930 --> 00:10:58,770
professional communication. We are going to talk about how to explain a project you worked on

232
00:10:58,770 --> 00:11:01,090
in 30 minutes during an interview.

233
00:11:01,090 --> 00:11:03,890
This is a very common interview situation.

234
00:11:03,890 --> 00:11:08,050
Many candidates struggle because they either speak too generally

235
00:11:08,050 --> 00:11:11,250
or they go into too much detail without structure.

236
00:11:11,250 --> 00:11:15,090
So today, we will show you exactly how to organize your answer

237
00:11:15,090 --> 00:11:18,450
in a clear, confident and professional way.

238
00:11:18,450 --> 00:11:22,050
We will also give you a realistic, long-form conversation

239
00:11:22,050 --> 00:11:23,810
just like a real interview.

240
00:11:23,810 --> 00:11:26,210
You will hear how to explain background,

241
00:11:26,210 --> 00:11:31,730
role, actions, challenges, and results naturally in English.

242
00:11:31,730 --> 00:11:36,850
And you can reuse these expressions in your own interviews, meetings, or presentations.

243
00:11:36,850 --> 00:11:38,250
So let's begin.

244
00:11:38,250 --> 00:11:42,170
So Sophia, let me ask you a common interview question.

245
00:11:42,170 --> 00:11:44,690
Can you tell me about a project you worked on recently?

246
00:11:44,690 --> 00:11:46,490
Yes, of course.

247
00:11:46,490 --> 00:11:51,530
One project I worked on recently was related to improving the customer onboarding process

248
00:11:51,530 --> 00:11:52,930
in our company.

249
00:11:52,930 --> 00:11:54,290
That sounds interesting.

250
00:11:54,290 --> 00:11:57,370
Can you start by giving me a simple overview of the project?

251
00:11:57,370 --> 00:11:58,370
Sure.

252
00:11:58,370 --> 00:12:04,090
The main goal of the project was to make the onboarding process faster, smoother, and easier

253
00:12:04,090 --> 00:12:09,930
for new customers, so that they could start using our service without confusion or delays.

254
00:12:09,930 --> 00:12:11,290
That's a very clear summary.

255
00:12:11,290 --> 00:12:14,090
I like that you started with the goal first.

256
00:12:14,090 --> 00:12:16,370
Now, let's go a bit deeper.

257
00:12:16,370 --> 00:12:19,810
What was the background or problem that led to this project?

258
00:12:19,810 --> 00:12:24,770
The background was that we noticed many new customers were dropping off during the onboarding

259
00:12:24,770 --> 00:12:25,770
stage.

260
00:12:25,770 --> 00:12:30,930
They were getting confused with the setup process and sometimes abandoning the platform before

261
00:12:30,930 --> 00:12:32,850
completing registration.

262
00:12:32,850 --> 00:12:35,890
So there was a clear problem with user experience.

263
00:12:35,890 --> 00:12:37,170
Exactly.

264
00:12:37,170 --> 00:12:41,610
Customer support tickets were increasing and feedback showed that people found the onboarding

265
00:12:41,610 --> 00:12:44,850
steps too complicated and time-consuming.

266
00:12:44,850 --> 00:12:46,010
That makes sense.

267
00:12:46,010 --> 00:12:48,670
So what was your role in this project?

268
00:12:48,670 --> 00:12:51,590
My role was project analyst and coordinator.

269
00:12:51,590 --> 00:12:57,390
I was responsible for collecting customer feedback, analyzing the onboarding flow, and working

270
00:12:57,390 --> 00:13:01,050
with the product and design teams to suggest improvements.

271
00:13:01,050 --> 00:13:03,290
That sounds like a very important role.

272
00:13:03,290 --> 00:13:05,470
So you were connecting different teams.

273
00:13:05,470 --> 00:13:06,810
Yes, exactly.

274
00:13:06,810 --> 00:13:11,730
I acted as a bridge between the customer insights team, the design team, and the

275
00:13:11,730 --> 00:13:13,070
technical team.

276
00:13:13,070 --> 00:13:17,390
My job was to make sure everyone understood the customer problems clearly.

277
00:13:17,390 --> 00:13:18,390
Great.

278
00:13:18,390 --> 00:13:21,990
Now let's talk about what you actually did, step by step.

279
00:13:21,990 --> 00:13:23,590
What was your first action?

280
00:13:23,590 --> 00:13:25,550
The first step was research.

281
00:13:25,550 --> 00:13:31,590
I collected data from customer surveys, support tickets, and user behavior tracking tools.

282
00:13:31,590 --> 00:13:35,990
I wanted to understand exactly where users were getting stuck.

283
00:13:35,990 --> 00:13:38,470
And what did you discover from that research?

284
00:13:38,470 --> 00:13:42,950
We discovered that most users were dropping off at the third step of the onboarding

285
00:13:42,950 --> 00:13:46,870
process, which required too much information at once.

286
00:13:46,870 --> 00:13:49,430
It was overwhelming for new users.

287
00:13:49,430 --> 00:13:51,710
That's a very important insight.

288
00:13:51,710 --> 00:13:54,630
What did you do after identifying that issue?

289
00:13:54,630 --> 00:13:59,750
After that, we mapped the entire onboarding journey and broke it into smaller, simpler

290
00:13:59,750 --> 00:14:00,750
steps.

291
00:14:00,750 --> 00:14:05,550
We redesigned the flow to make it more user-friendly and less time-consuming.

292
00:14:05,550 --> 00:14:08,910
Did you work closely with designers and developers at that stage?

293
00:14:08,910 --> 00:14:10,470
Yes, very closely.

294
00:14:10,470 --> 00:14:15,510
I organized weekly meetings with the UX design team and the development team to ensure

295
00:14:15,510 --> 00:14:20,070
the changes were technically possible and aligned with user needs.

296
00:14:20,070 --> 00:14:22,470
That sounds like strong collaboration.

297
00:14:22,470 --> 00:14:24,630
Were there any challenges during the project?

298
00:14:24,630 --> 00:14:26,270
Yes, definitely.

299
00:14:26,270 --> 00:14:29,870
One major challenge was that different teams had different opinions.

300
00:14:29,870 --> 00:14:34,230
The design team wanted simplicity, but the technical team was concerned about system

301
00:14:34,230 --> 00:14:35,230
limitations.

302
00:14:35,230 --> 00:14:37,870
That's a very common workplace challenge.

303
00:14:37,870 --> 00:14:39,110
How did you handle it?

304
00:14:39,110 --> 00:14:43,670
I organized alignment meetings, where each team explained their concerns.

305
00:14:43,670 --> 00:14:49,230
Then we focused on finding a balanced solution that worked for both usability and technical

306
00:14:49,230 --> 00:14:50,470
feasibility.

307
00:14:50,470 --> 00:14:52,990
That shows strong communication skills.

308
00:14:52,990 --> 00:14:54,750
Did anything else go wrong?

309
00:14:54,750 --> 00:14:57,670
Yes, another challenge was time pressure.

310
00:14:57,670 --> 00:15:01,870
We had a tight deadline because the company wanted to launch the new onboarding system

311
00:15:01,870 --> 00:15:04,350
before a new marketing campaign.

312
00:15:04,350 --> 00:15:06,670
So how did you manage that pressure?

313
00:15:06,670 --> 00:15:10,270
We prioritized the most critical changes first.

314
00:15:10,270 --> 00:15:15,130
Instead of redesigning everything, we focused on fixing the biggest drop-off points in the

315
00:15:15,130 --> 00:15:16,130
process.

316
00:15:16,130 --> 00:15:17,670
Very smart approach.

317
00:15:17,670 --> 00:15:20,410
Now let's move to the implementation phase.

318
00:15:20,410 --> 00:15:21,930
What happened next?

319
00:15:21,930 --> 00:15:26,130
After finalizing the design, the development team started building the new onboarding

320
00:15:26,130 --> 00:15:27,130
flow.

321
00:15:27,130 --> 00:15:31,890
I helped coordinate testing sessions and collected feedback from real users.

322
00:15:31,890 --> 00:15:34,530
Did you do any testing before full launch?

323
00:15:34,530 --> 00:15:38,810
Yes, we ran a pilot test with a small group of new users.

324
00:15:38,810 --> 00:15:43,850
This helped us identify small usability issues before rolling it out to everyone.

325
00:15:43,850 --> 00:15:46,770
That's a great example of continuous improvement.

326
00:15:46,770 --> 00:15:49,130
After that, did you launch it company-wide?

327
00:15:49,130 --> 00:15:50,130
Yes.

328
00:15:50,130 --> 00:15:54,810
After making final adjustments, we launched the new onboarding system globally.

329
00:15:54,810 --> 00:15:56,210
And what were the results?

330
00:15:56,210 --> 00:15:58,230
The results were very positive.

331
00:15:58,230 --> 00:16:03,290
The onboarding completion rate increased by 35 percent, and customer support tickets

332
00:16:03,290 --> 00:16:06,610
related to onboarding decreased significantly.

333
00:16:06,610 --> 00:16:08,290
That's a strong improvement.

334
00:16:08,290 --> 00:16:10,530
Did you receive any feedback from customers?

335
00:16:10,530 --> 00:16:16,690
Yes, customers said the new process felt faster, clearer, and much easier to follow.

336
00:16:16,690 --> 00:16:21,490
Many new users completed registration within minutes instead of abandoning it halfway.

337
00:16:21,490 --> 00:16:23,090
That's excellent.

338
00:16:23,090 --> 00:16:25,810
Now let's talk about your personal learning.

339
00:16:25,810 --> 00:16:27,650
What did you learn from this project?

340
00:16:27,650 --> 00:16:32,850
I learned the importance of understanding user behavior deeply before making changes.

341
00:16:32,850 --> 00:16:37,410
I also learned how important communication is between different departments.

342
00:16:37,410 --> 00:16:41,410
very valuable. Did this project change your skills in any way?

343
00:16:41,410 --> 00:16:49,410
Yes, I improved my data analysis skills, my communication skills, and my ability to manage cross-functional teams.

344
00:16:49,410 --> 00:16:57,410
Great! Now let's imagine you are in a real interview and the interviewer asks you to summarize the project in one minute. How would you answer?

345
00:16:57,410 --> 00:17:02,410
I would say I worked on improving the customer onboarding process.

346
00:17:02,410 --> 00:17:08,910
My role was to analyze user behavior and coordinate improvements with design and development teams.

347
00:17:08,910 --> 00:17:13,410
We identified major drop-off points and simplified the onboarding flow.

348
00:17:13,410 --> 00:17:21,410
After implementation, completion rates increased by 35% and customer satisfaction improved significantly.

349
00:17:21,410 --> 00:17:24,910
That's a perfect summary, very clear and professional.

350
00:17:24,910 --> 00:17:29,310
Thank you. In interviews, clarity is more important than complexity.

351
00:17:29,310 --> 00:17:34,410
Now let's talk about another example to help learners understand different project types.

352
00:17:34,410 --> 00:17:40,310
Good idea. Another project I worked on was improving internal communication within a company.

353
00:17:40,310 --> 00:17:42,410
What was the problem in that project?

354
00:17:42,410 --> 00:17:46,410
The problem was that employees were using different communication tools,

355
00:17:46,410 --> 00:17:49,310
which caused confusion and delayed responses.

356
00:17:49,310 --> 00:17:50,710
What was your role?

357
00:17:50,710 --> 00:17:57,110
I was responsible for analyzing communication gaps and proposing a centralized communication platform.

358
00:17:57,110 --> 00:17:58,710
What actions did you take?

359
00:17:58,710 --> 00:18:05,510
I interviewed employees, studied communication patterns, and worked with IT to evaluate different tools.

360
00:18:05,510 --> 00:18:06,710
Any challenges?

361
00:18:06,710 --> 00:18:12,910
Yes, resistance to change. Some employees preferred old systems and were hesitant to switch.

362
00:18:12,910 --> 00:18:14,410
How did you solve that?

363
00:18:14,410 --> 00:18:18,210
We organized training sessions and showed the benefits clearly.

364
00:18:18,210 --> 00:18:21,710
Gradually, employees adapted to the new system.

365
00:18:21,710 --> 00:18:22,710
And results?

366
00:18:22,710 --> 00:18:28,270
Communication became faster and more organized, and internal response time improved by around

367
00:18:28,270 --> 00:18:29,270
40%.

368
00:18:29,270 --> 00:18:30,270
Excellent.

369
00:18:30,270 --> 00:18:32,070
That's another strong example.

370
00:18:32,070 --> 00:18:34,630
Now let's give learners a final tip.

371
00:18:34,630 --> 00:18:39,030
When explaining a project in an interview, always remember this structure.

372
00:18:39,030 --> 00:18:44,550
Background, goal, role, actions, challenges, and results.

373
00:18:44,550 --> 00:18:45,550
Yes.

374
00:18:45,550 --> 00:18:47,830
And don't forget to use simple English.

375
00:18:47,830 --> 00:18:52,150
don't need advanced vocabulary, you need clear communication.

376
00:18:52,150 --> 00:18:55,330
Also always include real examples.

377
00:18:55,330 --> 00:19:00,210
Interviewers love specific details like numbers, improvements, and outcomes.

378
00:19:00,210 --> 00:19:02,650
And most importantly, stay calm.

379
00:19:02,650 --> 00:19:06,250
It's not a test of perfection, it's a test of communication.

380
00:19:06,250 --> 00:19:07,250
Exactly.

381
00:19:07,250 --> 00:19:12,030
If the interviewer understands your story clearly, you are already doing well.

382
00:19:12,030 --> 00:19:14,650
That brings us to the end of today's episode.

383
00:19:14,650 --> 00:19:16,470
Thank you so much for listening.

384
00:19:16,470 --> 00:19:20,110
We hope this helps you feel more confident in your next interview.

385
00:19:20,110 --> 00:19:24,070
Keep practicing, keep improving, and we'll see you in the next episode.

386
00:19:24,070 --> 00:19:25,070
Goodbye, everyone.

387
00:19:25,070 --> 00:19:26,070
Goodbye.

388
00:19:26,070 --> 00:19:29,230
Hello, and welcome to Business English Conversations.

389
00:19:29,230 --> 00:19:30,230
Hello, everyone.

390
00:19:30,230 --> 00:19:34,590
Welcome back to the podcast where we practice real English for the workplace.

391
00:19:34,590 --> 00:19:35,870
My name is Marco.

392
00:19:35,870 --> 00:19:36,990
And I'm Erica.

393
00:19:36,990 --> 00:19:40,190
We're really happy you're here learning with us today.

394
00:19:40,190 --> 00:19:45,270
In this podcast, we help you speak English naturally and confidently in professional

395
00:19:45,270 --> 00:19:49,870
situations like meetings, presentations, and job interviews.

396
00:19:49,870 --> 00:19:55,550
Yes, especially situations where you need to explain your experience clearly.

397
00:19:55,550 --> 00:19:58,670
Today we have a very important and practical topic.

398
00:19:58,670 --> 00:19:59,670
That's right.

399
00:19:59,670 --> 00:20:05,830
Today's topic is how to explain a project you worked on in 30 minutes during a job interview.

400
00:20:05,830 --> 00:20:07,870
This is a common situation.

401
00:20:07,870 --> 00:20:12,210
Many interviewers ask candidates to describe a project in detail.

402
00:20:12,210 --> 00:20:17,330
They want to understand not only what you did, but also how you think, how you solve

403
00:20:17,330 --> 00:20:19,850
problems, and how you communicate.

404
00:20:19,850 --> 00:20:20,850
Exactly.

405
00:20:20,850 --> 00:20:25,710
And sometimes, candidates feel nervous because 30 minutes is a long time.

406
00:20:25,710 --> 00:20:31,050
They worry about running out of things to say or not organizing their explanation well.

407
00:20:31,050 --> 00:20:34,530
But actually, 30 minutes is a great opportunity.

408
00:20:34,530 --> 00:20:39,250
It allows you to tell a complete story and show your skills in depth.

409
00:20:39,250 --> 00:20:44,450
In today's conversation, we will simulate a realistic interview scenario.

410
00:20:44,450 --> 00:20:50,410
Speaker 2 will act as the interviewer, and Speaker 1 will explain a project step by step

411
00:20:50,410 --> 00:20:52,770
in a clear and structured way.

412
00:20:52,770 --> 00:20:58,890
We will also naturally include useful phrases and examples that you can use in your own interviews.

413
00:20:58,890 --> 00:21:00,530
So let's begin.

414
00:21:00,530 --> 00:21:02,210
Thank you for joining us today.

415
00:21:02,210 --> 00:21:06,490
To start, I'd like you to tell me about a project you worked on recently.

416
00:21:06,490 --> 00:21:09,970
You can take your time and walk me through it in detail.

417
00:21:09,970 --> 00:21:10,970
Thank you.

418
00:21:10,970 --> 00:21:11,970
I'd be happy to.

419
00:21:11,970 --> 00:21:16,450
I'll explain one of the most important projects I worked on in my previous role.

420
00:21:16,450 --> 00:21:21,630
The project was about developing and launching a new internal dashboard system for tracking

421
00:21:21,630 --> 00:21:23,410
company performance.

422
00:21:23,410 --> 00:21:24,850
That sounds interesting.

423
00:21:24,850 --> 00:21:28,170
Could you start by explaining the background of the project?

424
00:21:28,170 --> 00:21:29,330
Of course.

425
00:21:29,330 --> 00:21:33,330
Before the project started, our company used several different tools to track

426
00:21:33,330 --> 00:21:34,890
performance data.

427
00:21:34,890 --> 00:21:39,330
Each department had its own system, which made it difficult to get a clear overall view

428
00:21:39,330 --> 00:21:40,730
of the business.

429
00:21:40,730 --> 00:21:43,810
So there was a lack of centralized data?

430
00:21:43,810 --> 00:21:45,170
Exactly.

431
00:21:45,170 --> 00:21:49,370
Managers had to collect reports manually from different teams, which was time consuming

432
00:21:49,370 --> 00:21:51,610
and sometimes inaccurate.

433
00:21:51,610 --> 00:21:54,290
Because of this, decision making was often delayed.

434
00:21:54,290 --> 00:21:55,370
I see.

435
00:21:55,370 --> 00:21:58,330
So what was the main goal of the project?

436
00:21:58,330 --> 00:22:03,050
The main goal was to create a centralized dashboard that would automatically collect

437
00:22:03,050 --> 00:22:06,730
and display key performance indicators in real time.

438
00:22:06,730 --> 00:22:08,650
This would help managers make faster

439
00:22:08,650 --> 00:22:10,690
and more informed decisions.

440
00:22:10,690 --> 00:22:13,170
That sounds like a valuable objective.

441
00:22:13,170 --> 00:22:15,390
What was your role in the project?

442
00:22:15,390 --> 00:22:17,490
My role was project lead.

443
00:22:17,490 --> 00:22:20,690
I was responsible for coordinating the entire project

444
00:22:20,690 --> 00:22:23,310
from planning to final implementation.

445
00:22:23,310 --> 00:22:26,450
Could you explain your responsibilities in more detail?

446
00:22:26,450 --> 00:22:30,230
Yes, I was responsible for defining the project's scope,

447
00:22:30,230 --> 00:22:33,010
setting timelines, managing communication

448
00:22:33,010 --> 00:22:36,370
between teams and ensuring that we met our deadlines.

449
00:22:36,370 --> 00:22:38,770
I also worked closely with both technical

450
00:22:38,770 --> 00:22:41,010
and non-technical stakeholders.

451
00:22:41,010 --> 00:22:42,930
So you had both management

452
00:22:42,930 --> 00:22:45,410
and communication responsibilities.

453
00:22:45,410 --> 00:22:46,310
That's right.

454
00:22:46,310 --> 00:22:49,890
A big part of my role was making sure everyone was aligned

455
00:22:49,890 --> 00:22:51,970
and understood the project goals.

456
00:22:51,970 --> 00:22:53,970
How did the project begin?

457
00:22:53,970 --> 00:22:56,730
The project began with a discovery phase.

458
00:22:56,730 --> 00:22:59,210
During this stage, we met with different departments

459
00:22:59,210 --> 00:23:01,730
to understand their needs and challenges.

460
00:23:01,730 --> 00:23:04,050
What kind of questions did you ask?

461
00:23:04,050 --> 00:23:07,170
We asked questions like, what data do you need daily?

462
00:23:07,170 --> 00:23:09,730
What problems do you face with the current system?

463
00:23:09,730 --> 00:23:12,770
And what features would help you work more efficiently?

464
00:23:12,770 --> 00:23:15,090
That sounds like a thorough approach.

465
00:23:15,090 --> 00:23:18,810
Yes, it was very important to gather detailed requirements.

466
00:23:18,810 --> 00:23:20,810
Without understanding user needs,

467
00:23:20,810 --> 00:23:23,330
we could not design an effective solution.

468
00:23:23,330 --> 00:23:26,210
What did you learn from this discovery phase?

469
00:23:26,210 --> 00:23:29,410
We learned that each department had different priorities,

470
00:23:29,410 --> 00:23:33,050
but they all needed faster access to accurate data.

471
00:23:33,050 --> 00:23:36,690
We also discovered that many processes were still manual.

472
00:23:36,690 --> 00:23:40,210
So automation was an important part of the solution.

473
00:23:40,210 --> 00:23:41,330
Exactly.

474
00:23:41,330 --> 00:23:43,250
After gathering all the information,

475
00:23:43,250 --> 00:23:45,330
we moved to the planning phase.

476
00:23:45,330 --> 00:23:47,650
What did the planning phase involve?

477
00:23:47,650 --> 00:23:48,730
In the planning phase,

478
00:23:48,730 --> 00:23:50,970
we defined the project timeline,

479
00:23:50,970 --> 00:23:54,090
allocated resources and created a roadmap.

480
00:23:54,090 --> 00:23:57,210
We also decided which technologies we would use.

481
00:23:57,210 --> 00:24:00,010
Did you face any challenges during planning?

482
00:24:00,010 --> 00:24:03,410
Yes, one challenge was balancing different requirements.

483
00:24:03,410 --> 00:24:06,250
Some departments wanted very detailed data,

484
00:24:06,250 --> 00:24:08,610
while others preferred a simple overview.

485
00:24:08,610 --> 00:24:10,210
How did you handle that?

486
00:24:10,210 --> 00:24:13,250
We designed a flexible dashboard that allowed users

487
00:24:13,250 --> 00:24:15,090
to customize what they see.

488
00:24:15,090 --> 00:24:17,370
This way, each department could use the system

489
00:24:17,370 --> 00:24:18,810
according to their needs.

490
00:24:18,810 --> 00:24:20,490
That's a smart solution.

491
00:24:20,490 --> 00:24:21,370
Thank you.

492
00:24:21,370 --> 00:24:24,530
After planning, we moved into the development phase.

493
00:24:24,530 --> 00:24:26,530
Could you describe that phase?

494
00:24:26,530 --> 00:24:30,330
During development, the technical team built the dashboard system.

495
00:24:30,330 --> 00:24:35,530
Meanwhile, I coordinated regular meetings to track progress and address any issues.

496
00:24:35,530 --> 00:24:37,210
How often did you meet?

497
00:24:37,210 --> 00:24:41,730
We had weekly progress meetings and daily check-ins during critical periods.

498
00:24:41,730 --> 00:24:43,850
That sounds very organized.

499
00:24:43,850 --> 00:24:46,210
Yes, communication was key.

500
00:24:46,210 --> 00:24:50,330
Without regular updates, small issues could become big problems.

501
00:24:50,330 --> 00:24:53,490
Were there any major challenges during development?

502
00:24:53,490 --> 00:24:57,170
Yes, one major challenge was data integration.

503
00:24:57,170 --> 00:24:59,210
Since data came from different systems,

504
00:24:59,210 --> 00:25:02,610
it was difficult to combine everything into one dashboard.

505
00:25:02,610 --> 00:25:04,850
I can imagine that was complex.

506
00:25:04,850 --> 00:25:05,730
It was.

507
00:25:05,730 --> 00:25:07,450
The data formats were different

508
00:25:07,450 --> 00:25:09,370
and some systems were outdated.

509
00:25:09,370 --> 00:25:11,850
How did your team solve this problem?

510
00:25:11,850 --> 00:25:14,930
The technical team developed custom integration tools

511
00:25:14,930 --> 00:25:17,250
and we worked closely with each department

512
00:25:17,250 --> 00:25:19,210
to ensure data accuracy.

513
00:25:19,210 --> 00:25:21,130
Did this affect your timeline?

514
00:25:21,130 --> 00:25:24,450
Yes, we had a slight delay of about two weeks.

515
00:25:24,450 --> 00:25:27,450
However, we communicated this clearly to stakeholders

516
00:25:27,450 --> 00:25:29,010
and adjusted our plan.

517
00:25:29,010 --> 00:25:31,330
That's important in project management.

518
00:25:31,330 --> 00:25:32,250
Absolutely.

519
00:25:32,250 --> 00:25:34,870
Transparency helps maintain trust.

520
00:25:34,870 --> 00:25:37,030
What happened after development?

521
00:25:37,030 --> 00:25:40,210
After development, we moved to the testing phase.

522
00:25:40,210 --> 00:25:42,530
What kind of testing did you perform?

523
00:25:42,530 --> 00:25:45,570
We conducted system testing, performance testing,

524
00:25:45,570 --> 00:25:47,650
and user acceptance testing.

525
00:25:47,650 --> 00:25:50,650
Could you explain user acceptance testing?

526
00:25:50,650 --> 00:25:55,010
It means real users test the system to see if it meets their needs.

527
00:25:55,010 --> 00:25:58,370
They provide feedback on usability and functionality.

528
00:25:58,370 --> 00:26:00,450
What feedback did you receive?

529
00:26:00,450 --> 00:26:04,090
Some users found the interface slightly complex at first,

530
00:26:04,090 --> 00:26:08,170
so we simplified the design and added tool tips to guide users.

531
00:26:08,170 --> 00:26:11,330
That's a good example of improving based on feedback.

532
00:26:11,330 --> 00:26:14,290
Yes, user feedback was very valuable.

533
00:26:14,290 --> 00:26:17,330
How long did the entire project take?

534
00:26:17,330 --> 00:26:20,730
The project took about five months from start to finish.

535
00:26:20,730 --> 00:26:23,610
And what happened during the final stage?

536
00:26:23,610 --> 00:26:25,970
The final stage was implementation.

537
00:26:25,970 --> 00:26:28,450
We rolled out the dashboard to all departments

538
00:26:28,450 --> 00:26:30,370
and provided training sessions.

539
00:26:30,370 --> 00:26:32,610
Training is often overlooked.

540
00:26:32,610 --> 00:26:34,290
Yes, but it's essential.

541
00:26:34,290 --> 00:26:36,410
Even the best system won't be effective

542
00:26:36,410 --> 00:26:38,330
if people don't know how to use it.

543
00:26:38,330 --> 00:26:41,330
How did employees respond to the new system?

544
00:26:41,330 --> 00:26:43,330
At first, some were hesitant,

545
00:26:43,330 --> 00:26:45,930
but after training, they found it very useful.

546
00:26:45,930 --> 00:26:47,330
That's good to hear.

547
00:26:47,330 --> 00:26:50,010
What were the final results of the project?

548
00:26:50,010 --> 00:26:51,890
The results were very positive.

549
00:26:51,890 --> 00:26:54,490
Reporting time decreased by 40%

550
00:26:54,490 --> 00:26:57,990
and managers could access real-time data instantly.

551
00:26:57,990 --> 00:27:00,170
That's a significant improvement.

552
00:27:00,170 --> 00:27:04,030
Yes, and decision-making became faster and more accurate.

553
00:27:04,030 --> 00:27:06,430
Did you measure user satisfaction?

554
00:27:06,430 --> 00:27:08,210
Yes, we conducted a survey

555
00:27:08,210 --> 00:27:12,410
and over 85% of users reported improved efficiency.

556
00:27:12,410 --> 00:27:13,650
That's excellent.

557
00:27:13,650 --> 00:27:14,490
Thank you.

558
00:27:14,490 --> 00:27:18,210
The project also improved collaboration between departments.

559
00:27:18,210 --> 00:27:21,370
Looking back, what was the biggest challenge?

560
00:27:21,370 --> 00:27:26,250
The biggest challenge was aligning different teams and managing expectations.

561
00:27:26,250 --> 00:27:28,570
And what was your biggest achievement?

562
00:27:28,570 --> 00:27:34,330
Successfully delivering a system that met diverse needs and created measurable impact.

563
00:27:34,330 --> 00:27:36,490
What did you learn from this experience?

564
00:27:36,490 --> 00:27:41,510
I learned the importance of communication, flexibility, and user involvement.

565
00:27:41,510 --> 00:27:44,950
If you had to do the project again, what would you change?

566
00:27:44,950 --> 00:27:49,910
I would involve end users earlier in the design phase to reduce revisions later.

567
00:27:49,910 --> 00:27:51,950
That's a valuable insight.

568
00:27:51,950 --> 00:27:54,990
Yes, early feedback can save time.

569
00:27:54,990 --> 00:27:58,110
Thank you so much for the detailed explanation.

570
00:27:58,110 --> 00:28:01,350
Before we finish, could you summarize the project briefly?

571
00:28:01,350 --> 00:28:02,350
Sure.

572
00:28:02,350 --> 00:28:06,470
The project focused on building a centralized performance dashboard.

573
00:28:06,470 --> 00:28:08,510
My role was Project Lead.

574
00:28:08,510 --> 00:28:13,510
We identified problems, designed a solution, overcame technical challenges, and delivered

575
00:28:13,510 --> 00:28:17,070
a system that improved efficiency and decision-making.

576
00:28:17,070 --> 00:28:20,350
That was a very clear and structured explanation.

577
00:28:20,350 --> 00:28:21,350
Thank you.

578
00:28:21,350 --> 00:28:25,670
This example shows how you can effectively use 30 minutes in an interview.

579
00:28:25,670 --> 00:28:32,510
You can explain the background, goal, role, process, challenges, and results in detail.

580
00:28:32,510 --> 00:28:36,510
Yes, and the key is to tell the story step by step.

581
00:28:36,510 --> 00:28:37,510
Exactly.

582
00:28:37,510 --> 00:28:42,570
Don't rush, use simple, clear language, and focus on your contribution.

583
00:28:42,570 --> 00:28:45,650
And always include results and lessons learned.

584
00:28:45,650 --> 00:28:47,090
That's right.

585
00:28:47,090 --> 00:28:49,730
Interviewers want to see impact and growth.

586
00:28:49,730 --> 00:28:53,010
Before we end, let's give our listeners a practice question.

587
00:28:53,010 --> 00:28:54,010
Good idea.

588
00:28:54,010 --> 00:28:55,690
Here is your question.

589
00:28:55,690 --> 00:29:00,650
Can you explain a project you worked on for about 10 to 15 minutes?

590
00:29:00,650 --> 00:29:05,610
Start with the background, explain your role, describe the process, talk about challenges

591
00:29:05,610 --> 00:29:07,490
and finish with results.

592
00:29:07,490 --> 00:29:13,210
Then try to expand it to 30 minutes by adding more details and examples.

593
00:29:13,210 --> 00:29:14,490
Practice speaking out loud.

594
00:29:14,490 --> 00:29:16,730
This will help you become more confident.

595
00:29:16,730 --> 00:29:18,930
Thank you so much for listening today.

596
00:29:18,930 --> 00:29:22,610
We hope this conversation helps you succeed in your interviews.

597
00:29:22,610 --> 00:29:26,610
Keep practicing, keep improving, and keep speaking English.

598
00:29:26,610 --> 00:29:27,690
See you next time.

599
00:29:27,690 --> 00:29:28,970
Goodbye, everyone.

600
00:29:28,970 --> 00:29:31,970
Hello, and welcome back to Business English.

601
00:29:31,970 --> 00:29:33,250
Hi, everyone.

602
00:29:33,250 --> 00:29:35,970
Welcome to today's English Conversation Practice.

603
00:29:35,970 --> 00:29:37,170
I'm Daniel.

604
00:29:37,170 --> 00:29:41,130
And I'm Sophia, and we're really happy you're here learning with us today.

605
00:29:41,130 --> 00:29:46,570
On this channel, we help you speak English naturally and confidently through real conversations,

606
00:29:46,570 --> 00:29:50,570
practical examples, and everyday workplace situations.

607
00:29:50,570 --> 00:29:55,090
Today's episode is especially useful for job interviews, because we are going to practice

608
00:29:55,090 --> 00:29:59,530
how to explain a project you worked on in 30 minutes during an interview.

609
00:29:59,530 --> 00:30:02,750
Yes, this is a very important skill.

610
00:30:02,750 --> 00:30:07,630
Many candidates fail not because they don't have experience, but because they cannot explain

611
00:30:07,630 --> 00:30:10,070
their project clearly under pressure.

612
00:30:10,070 --> 00:30:15,790
So today, we are going to show you a long, realistic interview conversation with a strong

613
00:30:15,790 --> 00:30:16,790
structure.

614
00:30:16,790 --> 00:30:17,790
Exactly.

615
00:30:17,790 --> 00:30:21,390
We will show you how to organize your answers step by step.

616
00:30:21,390 --> 00:30:27,950
Background, problem, role, actions, challenges, solutions, and results.

617
00:30:27,950 --> 00:30:30,830
This is exactly what interviewers want to hear.

618
00:30:30,830 --> 00:30:32,630
Alright, let's begin.

619
00:30:32,630 --> 00:30:35,070
Imagine this is a real job interview.

620
00:30:35,070 --> 00:30:37,910
I am the interviewer and you are the candidate.

621
00:30:37,910 --> 00:30:38,750
Perfect.

622
00:30:38,750 --> 00:30:42,510
I'll do my best to stay clear, structured and professional.

623
00:30:42,510 --> 00:30:43,350
Great.

624
00:30:43,350 --> 00:30:44,550
So let's start.

625
00:30:44,550 --> 00:30:47,710
Can you tell me about a project you worked on recently?

626
00:30:47,710 --> 00:30:49,030
Yes, of course.

627
00:30:49,030 --> 00:30:51,550
One of the most important projects I worked on

628
00:30:51,550 --> 00:30:54,590
was an internal customer experience improvement

629
00:30:54,590 --> 00:30:56,910
project at my previous company.

630
00:30:56,910 --> 00:30:59,670
The goal of the project was to improve the speed

631
00:30:59,670 --> 00:31:01,830
and quality of customer support

632
00:31:01,830 --> 00:31:05,510
by redesigning our internal ticket handling system.

633
00:31:05,510 --> 00:31:07,190
Okay, good start.

634
00:31:07,190 --> 00:31:08,710
Before we go deeper,

635
00:31:08,710 --> 00:31:12,310
can you explain the situation before the project started?

636
00:31:12,310 --> 00:31:14,750
Yes, before the project started,

637
00:31:14,750 --> 00:31:18,350
our customer support team was facing several issues.

638
00:31:18,350 --> 00:31:20,450
We were receiving a very high number

639
00:31:20,450 --> 00:31:22,570
of customer inquiries every day,

640
00:31:22,570 --> 00:31:25,150
but the system we used to manage those inquiries

641
00:31:25,150 --> 00:31:26,610
was not efficient.

642
00:31:26,610 --> 00:31:28,490
Tickets were often delayed,

643
00:31:28,490 --> 00:31:33,690
Some were assigned incorrectly, and customers had to wait too long for responses.

644
00:31:33,690 --> 00:31:39,450
In some cases, response time was more than 24 hours, which led to customer dissatisfaction

645
00:31:39,450 --> 00:31:41,370
and negative feedback.

646
00:31:41,370 --> 00:31:42,450
I see.

647
00:31:42,450 --> 00:31:45,330
So what was your role in this situation?

648
00:31:45,330 --> 00:31:47,690
My role was Project Coordinator.

649
00:31:47,690 --> 00:31:53,330
I was responsible for organizing communication between different departments, tracking progress,

650
00:31:53,330 --> 00:31:56,610
and making sure everyone understood the project goals.

651
00:31:56,610 --> 00:32:01,410
I worked closely with the customer support team, the IT department, and the operations

652
00:32:01,410 --> 00:32:02,510
team.

653
00:32:02,510 --> 00:32:05,130
That sounds like a cross-functional role.

654
00:32:05,130 --> 00:32:07,570
What was the main goal of the project?

655
00:32:07,570 --> 00:32:13,930
The main goal was to reduce customer response time from over 24 hours to under 6 hours.

656
00:32:13,930 --> 00:32:19,530
We also wanted to improve ticket accuracy, reduce manual work, and increase customer

657
00:32:19,530 --> 00:32:20,970
satisfaction.

658
00:32:20,970 --> 00:32:23,490
That sounds like a big improvement target.

659
00:32:23,490 --> 00:32:25,470
How did you begin the project?

660
00:32:25,470 --> 00:32:28,790
We started with a planning and analysis phase.

661
00:32:28,790 --> 00:32:32,350
First we collected data from the existing customer support system.

662
00:32:32,350 --> 00:32:38,510
We analyzed response times, ticket volumes, customer complaints, and resolution rates.

663
00:32:38,510 --> 00:32:42,230
Then we identified the main bottlenecks in the system.

664
00:32:42,230 --> 00:32:44,950
And what did you find during that analysis?

665
00:32:44,950 --> 00:32:47,350
We found three major problems.

666
00:32:47,350 --> 00:32:52,850
First, tickets were not being categorized properly, so simple questions and complex

667
00:32:52,850 --> 00:32:54,510
issues were mixed together.

668
00:32:54,510 --> 00:33:00,790
Second, there was no proper prioritization system, so urgent requests were sometimes delayed.

669
00:33:00,790 --> 00:33:07,350
Third, a large amount of manual work was required, which slowed down the entire process.

670
00:33:07,350 --> 00:33:08,470
That makes sense.

671
00:33:08,470 --> 00:33:10,970
So what solution did you propose?

672
00:33:10,970 --> 00:33:15,230
We proposed implementing a new automated ticket management system.

673
00:33:15,230 --> 00:33:20,330
The system would automatically categorize incoming customer requests based on keywords

674
00:33:20,330 --> 00:33:21,690
and urgency.

675
00:33:21,690 --> 00:33:27,270
It would also assign tickets to the correct department and prioritize urgent cases.

676
00:33:27,270 --> 00:33:30,490
Were you involved in the technical development of that system?

677
00:33:30,490 --> 00:33:36,390
I was not directly involved in coding or software development, but I played a very important

678
00:33:36,390 --> 00:33:37,770
coordination role.

679
00:33:37,770 --> 00:33:41,730
I worked as a bridge between the business teams and the IT team.

680
00:33:41,730 --> 00:33:46,930
I translated business requirements into clear instructions for developers and ensured

681
00:33:46,930 --> 00:33:50,170
the system met the needs of customer service users.

682
00:33:50,170 --> 00:33:51,170
Good.

683
00:33:51,170 --> 00:33:54,210
How did collaboration work between teams?

684
00:33:54,210 --> 00:33:57,170
We had weekly meetings with all stakeholders.

685
00:33:57,170 --> 00:34:02,810
The IT team handled development, the customer support team provided real user feedback,

686
00:34:02,810 --> 00:34:06,850
and the operations team helped us understand workflow requirements.

687
00:34:06,850 --> 00:34:12,250
My job was to make sure communication was clear and consistent between all groups.

688
00:34:12,250 --> 00:34:14,710
That sounds like a lot of responsibility.

689
00:34:14,710 --> 00:34:17,530
What was one of the first challenges you faced?

690
00:34:17,530 --> 00:34:22,250
One of the first challenges was that different departments had different expectations.

691
00:34:22,250 --> 00:34:27,290
The IT team wanted a simple system that was easy to build, while the customer support team

692
00:34:27,290 --> 00:34:30,810
wanted advanced features to make their job easier.

693
00:34:30,810 --> 00:34:33,850
These expectations sometimes conflicted.

694
00:34:33,850 --> 00:34:36,050
How did you manage that conflict?

695
00:34:36,050 --> 00:34:40,930
I organized alignment meetings where each team could explain their needs.

696
00:34:40,930 --> 00:34:46,170
Instead of focusing on individual preferences, we focused on the overall business goal,

697
00:34:46,170 --> 00:34:48,850
which was improving customer response time.

698
00:34:48,850 --> 00:34:52,370
We prioritized features that had the highest impact.

699
00:34:52,370 --> 00:34:53,730
That's a good approach.

700
00:34:53,730 --> 00:34:55,570
Were there any other challenges?

701
00:34:55,570 --> 00:34:59,670
Yes, another major challenge was resistance to change.

702
00:34:59,670 --> 00:35:03,410
Some employees were very comfortable with the old system and were worried that the

703
00:35:03,410 --> 00:35:07,250
new system would be complicated or increase their workload.

704
00:35:07,250 --> 00:35:11,450
They were also afraid of making mistakes while learning the new system.

705
00:35:11,450 --> 00:35:13,710
How did you handle that resistance?

706
00:35:13,710 --> 00:35:18,310
We introduced training sessions and created step-by-step user guides.

707
00:35:18,310 --> 00:35:23,310
We also organized hands-on workshops where employees could practice using the new system

708
00:35:23,310 --> 00:35:25,150
in a safe environment.

709
00:35:25,150 --> 00:35:29,190
Over time, as they became more comfortable, their resistance decreased.

710
00:35:29,190 --> 00:35:30,590
Good.

711
00:35:30,590 --> 00:35:32,310
What about technical challenges?

712
00:35:32,310 --> 00:35:35,710
Yes, there were technical challenges as well.

713
00:35:35,710 --> 00:35:40,590
One of the biggest issues was integrating the new ticket system with the existing customer

714
00:35:40,590 --> 00:35:41,590
database.

715
00:35:41,590 --> 00:35:48,590
During testing, we discovered some data synchronization issues, which caused delays in updating ticket status.

716
00:35:48,590 --> 00:35:50,590
Did that affect your timeline?

717
00:35:50,590 --> 00:35:54,590
Yes, slightly. The project was delayed by about two weeks.

718
00:35:54,590 --> 00:36:00,590
However, we decided not to rush the process because we wanted to ensure system stability and accuracy.

719
00:36:00,590 --> 00:36:03,590
In the end, management supported this decision.

720
00:36:03,590 --> 00:36:09,590
That's good. Now, let's talk about the testing phase. What happened after development?

721
00:36:09,590 --> 00:36:12,720
After development, we conducted a pilot test.

722
00:36:12,720 --> 00:36:18,520
We selected a small group of customer support agents to use the new system for three weeks.

723
00:36:18,520 --> 00:36:23,420
During this time, we collected feedback and monitored system performance closely.

724
00:36:23,420 --> 00:36:25,720
What kind of feedback did you receive?

725
00:36:25,720 --> 00:36:28,020
The feedback was mostly positive.

726
00:36:28,020 --> 00:36:32,520
Users liked the faster ticket assignment and improved organization.

727
00:36:32,520 --> 00:36:35,920
However, they also pointed out some usability issues,

728
00:36:35,920 --> 00:36:40,920
such as unclear navigation and too many steps for certain tasks.

729
00:36:41,040 --> 00:36:43,440
How did you respond to that feedback?

730
00:36:43,440 --> 00:36:46,720
The IT team made improvements based on the feedback.

731
00:36:46,720 --> 00:36:50,560
We simplified the interface, reduced unnecessary steps,

732
00:36:50,560 --> 00:36:53,000
and improved the dashboard layout.

733
00:36:53,000 --> 00:36:54,200
After these adjustments,

734
00:36:54,200 --> 00:36:57,080
the system became much more user-friendly.

735
00:36:57,080 --> 00:36:59,400
After the pilot, what happened next?

736
00:36:59,400 --> 00:37:00,600
After the pilot phase,

737
00:37:00,600 --> 00:37:03,040
we rolled out the system company-wide.

738
00:37:03,040 --> 00:37:06,600
We provided training sessions for all customer support staff

739
00:37:06,600 --> 00:37:10,840
and made sure everyone understood how to use the system effectively.

740
00:37:10,840 --> 00:37:14,520
And how did employees react after full implementation?

741
00:37:14,520 --> 00:37:17,440
Initially, some employees were still cautious,

742
00:37:17,440 --> 00:37:21,200
but within a few weeks, most people adapted to the new system.

743
00:37:21,200 --> 00:37:22,920
They started to see the benefits,

744
00:37:22,920 --> 00:37:26,880
especially in reduced workload and faster response times.

745
00:37:26,880 --> 00:37:28,720
Let's talk about results.

746
00:37:28,720 --> 00:37:31,560
What were the final outcomes of the project?

747
00:37:31,560 --> 00:37:33,760
The results were very positive.

748
00:37:33,760 --> 00:37:36,320
We reduced average customer response time

749
00:37:36,320 --> 00:37:39,880
from over 24 hours to around five hours.

750
00:37:39,880 --> 00:37:43,000
Ticket processing efficiency improved significantly

751
00:37:43,000 --> 00:37:47,280
and manual workload decreased by nearly 40%.

752
00:37:47,280 --> 00:37:48,940
That's a big improvement.

753
00:37:48,940 --> 00:37:51,200
What about customer satisfaction?

754
00:37:51,200 --> 00:37:53,520
Customer satisfaction scores increased

755
00:37:53,520 --> 00:37:56,380
by about 30% after implementation.

756
00:37:56,380 --> 00:37:59,080
We also saw a reduction in customer complaints

757
00:37:59,080 --> 00:38:01,440
related to delayed responses.

758
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:05,020
That's excellent. Did the company recognize your work?

759
00:38:05,020 --> 00:38:08,340
Yes, the project was recognized by senior management.

760
00:38:08,340 --> 00:38:10,540
Our team received positive feedback

761
00:38:10,540 --> 00:38:16,140
and the project was considered a successful digital transformation initiative within the company.

762
00:38:16,140 --> 00:38:19,500
Looking back, what did you learn from this project?

763
00:38:19,500 --> 00:38:24,400
I learned that communication is the most important factor in any project.

764
00:38:24,400 --> 00:38:29,260
Even with good technical solutions, without clear communication between teams,

765
00:38:29,260 --> 00:38:31,900
the project can face serious issues.

766
00:38:31,900 --> 00:38:35,620
I also learned the importance of managing change carefully

767
00:38:35,620 --> 00:38:37,700
because people need time and support

768
00:38:37,700 --> 00:38:39,900
to adapt to new systems.

769
00:38:39,900 --> 00:38:41,380
Very insightful.

770
00:38:41,380 --> 00:38:43,820
Now, if you had to do the project again,

771
00:38:43,820 --> 00:38:45,580
what would you do differently?

772
00:38:45,580 --> 00:38:49,260
I would involve end users earlier in the design phase.

773
00:38:49,260 --> 00:38:50,820
Their feedback would have helped us

774
00:38:50,820 --> 00:38:53,340
identify usability issues sooner.

775
00:38:53,340 --> 00:38:56,100
I would also spend more time on initial planning

776
00:38:56,100 --> 00:38:59,140
to reduce small delays during development.

777
00:38:59,140 --> 00:39:00,860
That's a strong reflection.

778
00:39:00,860 --> 00:39:02,860
Now, one final question.

779
00:39:02,860 --> 00:39:04,860
How would you summarize this project

780
00:39:04,860 --> 00:39:07,180
in a short answer for an interview?

781
00:39:07,180 --> 00:39:10,820
I would say I worked on a customer experience improvement

782
00:39:10,820 --> 00:39:14,540
project where I coordinated between IT and customer support

783
00:39:14,540 --> 00:39:17,940
teams to implement a new ticket management system.

784
00:39:17,940 --> 00:39:21,620
The project reduced response time from 24 hours to five

785
00:39:21,620 --> 00:39:24,780
hours, improved efficiency, and increased customer

786
00:39:24,780 --> 00:39:27,060
satisfaction by 30%.

787
00:39:27,060 --> 00:39:30,220
my role focused on coordination, communication,

788
